## The Numbers Don't Lie: A Surge in Recalls

With 327 food recalls already this year, the numbers are pointing to a troubling trend. To put things in perspective, there have been a total of 685 recalls to date, which means we've already crossed the halfway mark just a third of the way through the year. Of these, **90 were deemed Class I**, indicating a significant risk of serious health consequences or death.

## What's Going Wrong?

This year alone, 184 recalls have been due to allergens, 106 to salmonella, and 80 to foreign objects found in food products. One of the most acute concerns is mislabeling, with 4 recalls attributed to this seemingly small but potentially dangerous error. Listeria and E. coli also make their unfortunate appearances, with 113 and 9 incidents respectively.

## Repeat Offenders: The Usual Suspects

Among the repeat offenders, several brands stand out for their multiple infractions. Notably, Moonlight Packing Corporation and Albertsons Companies LLC have both been flagged with 11 recalls each, all of which were Class I. Brands like CHETAK NEW YORK LLC and Made Fresh Salads Inc. also make the list with numerous recalls, all Class I.

## The Latest Round of Trouble

The most recent recalls as of April 29, 2026, include some familiar names. Costco Wholesale Corporation had to recall their Kirkland Signature Traditional Madeleines due to undeclared hazelnut and soy lecithin. Ethnic Foods Inc. faced multiple recalls for undeclared artificial food colors in their products. Summit Import Corporation found lead levels exceeding acceptable standards in their spice powder. Finally, Unistel Industries Inc. recalled their ground allspice due to salmonella contamination.

## What Can You Do?

As a consumer, staying informed is your best defense. Regularly check updated recall lists and familiarize yourself with the common culprits. Inspect product labels carefully and heed any recall notices for products you may have at home. It is better to be proactive than to suffer the consequences of a contaminated or mislabeled food product.
